rgb.photo.v1.929a5ab4-779f-41ed-8d36-4dc601d6b4be	rgb.user.v1.ffb2fde6-fba6-40aa-8bb6-55b23d5d3c1d	photo	2017-03-19 08:49:22+00	2020-03-15 18:18:40.364+00	3	3487	5231	#28111f	{"bucket": "charliemcgrady-media-prod", "suffixes": {"lg": {"jpg": "_lg.jpg", "webp": "_lg.webp"}, "md": {"jpg": "_md.jpg", "webp": "_md.webp"}, "sm": {"jpg": "_sm.jpg", "webp": "_sm.webp"}, "xl": {"jpg": "_xl.jpg", "webp": "_xl.webp"}, "xs": {"jpg": "_xs.jpg", "webp": "_xs.webp"}, "xxl": {"jpg": "_xxl.jpg", "webp": "_xxl.webp"}, "xxs": {"jpg": "_xxs.jpg", "webp": "_xxs.webp"}}, "keyPrefix": "rgb.user.v1.ffb2fde6-fba6-40aa-8bb6-55b23d5d3c1d/rgb.photo.v1.929a5ab4-779f-41ed-8d36-4dc601d6b4be/rgb.photo.v1.929a5ab4-779f-41ed-8d36-4dc601d6b4be"}	\N
rgb.photo.v1.5489634c-ab37-4418-90f0-b2d90067c4b9	rgb.user.v1.ffb2fde6-fba6-40aa-8bb6-55b23d5d3c1d	photo	2019-06-16 02:22:02+00	2020-03-15 18:18:40.703+00	3	3753	5626	#5b4f52	{"bucket": "charliemcgrady-media-prod", "suffixes": {"lg": {"jpg": "_lg.jpg", "webp": "_lg.webp"}, "md": {"jpg": "_md.jpg", "webp": "_md.webp"}, "sm": {"jpg": "_sm.jpg", "webp": "_sm.webp"}, "xl": {"jpg": "_xl.jpg", "webp": "_xl.webp"}, "xs": {"jpg": "_xs.jpg", "webp": "_xs.webp"}, "xxl": {"jpg": "_xxl.jpg", "webp": "_xxl.webp"}, "xxs": {"jpg": "_xxs.jpg", "webp": "_xxs.webp"}}, "keyPrefix": "rgb.user.v1.ffb2fde6-fba6-40aa-8bb6-55b23d5d3c1d/rgb.photo.v1.5489634c-ab37-4418-90f0-b2d90067c4b9/rgb.photo.v1.5489634c-ab37-4418-90f0-b2d90067c4b9"}	<p>Pat - a legendary instructor for the Washington Alpine Club - and I were on "sweep duty" that morning. We were the last rope team up, and were responsible for handling any evacuations due to altitude sickness or exhaustion. </p><p><br></p><p>If you look closely on the rope, you will see butterfly knots. These knots are intended to catch on the lip of a crevasse if one of us fell in. We took this extra precaution because we were a rope team of two. Normally, you climb glaciers with teams of 3 or 4 to make self-rescue out of a crevasse easier.</p>
rgb.photo.v1.c65470f0-bd4c-41e3-9dc3-3d4cd3e59302	rgb.user.v1.ffb2fde6-fba6-40aa-8bb6-55b23d5d3c1d	photo	2019-11-23 17:31:17+00	2020-03-09 16:48:42.072+00	3	5805	3870	#a093af	{"bucket": "charliemcgrady-media-prod", "suffixes": {"lg": {"jpg": "_lg.jpg", "webp": "_lg.webp"}, "md": {"jpg": "_md.jpg", "webp": "_md.webp"}, "sm": {"jpg": "_sm.jpg", "webp": "_sm.webp"}, "xl": {"jpg": "_xl.jpg", "webp": "_xl.webp"}, "xs": {"jpg": "_xs.jpg", "webp": "_xs.webp"}, "xxl": {"jpg": "_xxl.jpg", "webp": "_xxl.webp"}, "xxs": {"jpg": "_xxs.jpg", "webp": "_xxs.webp"}}, "keyPrefix": "rgb.user.v1.ffb2fde6-fba6-40aa-8bb6-55b23d5d3c1d/rgb.photo.v1.c65470f0-bd4c-41e3-9dc3-3d4cd3e59302/rgb.photo.v1.c65470f0-bd4c-41e3-9dc3-3d4cd3e59302"}	If I had one word to describe the Jampa Glacier, it would be relentless. The glacier maintains a steady 45 degree slope for almost a mile of vertical elevation. On Mount Rainier, there is micro-terrain where you can put your pack down and get a brief respite from the uphill march. No such flatness exists on the Jampa. Instead, we resorted to cutting ice-benches with our axes. These seats did not provide much of a break; clinging to your bag while looking 3000 ft down an icy slope isn't exactly relaxing.
rgb.photo.v1.3296c407-7ccc-4ebe-b45c-914dba4a9fe8	rgb.user.v1.ffb2fde6-fba6-40aa-8bb6-55b23d5d3c1d	photo	2019-11-23 17:29:20+00	2020-03-09 19:27:32.119+00	3	2943	1962	#44383c	{"bucket": "charliemcgrady-media-prod", "suffixes": {"lg": {"jpg": "_lg.jpg", "webp": "_lg.webp"}, "md": {"jpg": "_md.jpg", "webp": "_md.webp"}, "sm": {"jpg": "_sm.jpg", "webp": "_sm.webp"}, "xl": {"jpg": "_xl.jpg", "webp": "_xl.webp"}, "xs": {"jpg": "_xs.jpg", "webp": "_xs.webp"}, "xxl": {"jpg": "_xxl.jpg", "webp": "_xxl.webp"}, "xxs": {"jpg": "_xxs.jpg", "webp": "_xxs.webp"}}, "keyPrefix": "rgb.user.v1.ffb2fde6-fba6-40aa-8bb6-55b23d5d3c1d/rgb.photo.v1.3296c407-7ccc-4ebe-b45c-914dba4a9fe8/rgb.photo.v1.3296c407-7ccc-4ebe-b45c-914dba4a9fe8"}	The realities of mountaineering - When sharing mountaineering photos, it's tempting to only share the glorious, testosterone-filled summit photos. I think this picture is a much more accurate portrait of high-altitude climbing. 5 steps forward followed by a minute of panic-stricken breaths, attempting to suck in as much energy before the next 15 second uphill battle.
rgb.photo.v1.6f4daa04-1dc9-41c7-8c7a-47f46fcbfed6	rgb.user.v1.ffb2fde6-fba6-40aa-8bb6-55b23d5d3c1d	photo	2019-11-23 16:10:17+00	2020-03-09 16:52:09.093+00	3	6000	4000	#51504e	{"bucket": "charliemcgrady-media-prod", "suffixes": {"lg": {"jpg": "_lg.jpg", "webp": "_lg.webp"}, "md": {"jpg": "_md.jpg", "webp": "_md.webp"}, "sm": {"jpg": "_sm.jpg", "webp": "_sm.webp"}, "xl": {"jpg": "_xl.jpg", "webp": "_xl.webp"}, "xs": {"jpg": "_xs.jpg", "webp": "_xs.webp"}, "xxl": {"jpg": "_xxl.jpg", "webp": "_xxl.webp"}, "xxs": {"jpg": "_xxs.jpg", "webp": "_xxs.webp"}}, "keyPrefix": "rgb.user.v1.ffb2fde6-fba6-40aa-8bb6-55b23d5d3c1d/rgb.photo.v1.6f4daa04-1dc9-41c7-8c7a-47f46fcbfed6/rgb.photo.v1.6f4daa04-1dc9-41c7-8c7a-47f46fcbfed6"}	Having just fought our way through a mile of boulders and ice called the Labyrinth, we got sight of our objective - Pico de Orizaba, guarded by the Jampa glacier. There's a lot of energy that goes into shots like these. I never climb with a tripod, so I spent a couple of minutes clambering with rocks to create a stable platform for my camera. I then spent 5 minutes setting the exposure and experimentally framing the shot (you cannot see through the viewfinder at night). After I got my exposure, I realized that our break was over before I even took a sip of water. Such is the life of photography on big-mountain climbs.
rgb.photo.v1.16a4fb9f-cc7a-4740-9a3c-dcc0cfcb426f	rgb.user.v1.ffb2fde6-fba6-40aa-8bb6-55b23d5d3c1d	photo	2019-11-23 17:57:35+00	2020-03-09 16:48:01.201+00	3	3712	2475	#395872	{"bucket": "charliemcgrady-media-prod", "suffixes": {"lg": {"jpg": "_lg.jpg", "webp": "_lg.webp"}, "md": {"jpg": "_md.jpg", "webp": "_md.webp"}, "sm": {"jpg": "_sm.jpg", "webp": "_sm.webp"}, "xl": {"jpg": "_xl.jpg", "webp": "_xl.webp"}, "xs": {"jpg": "_xs.jpg", "webp": "_xs.webp"}, "xxl": {"jpg": "_xxl.jpg", "webp": "_xxl.webp"}, "xxs": {"jpg": "_xxs.jpg", "webp": "_xxs.webp"}}, "keyPrefix": "rgb.user.v1.ffb2fde6-fba6-40aa-8bb6-55b23d5d3c1d/rgb.photo.v1.16a4fb9f-cc7a-4740-9a3c-dcc0cfcb426f/rgb.photo.v1.16a4fb9f-cc7a-4740-9a3c-dcc0cfcb426f"}	There are thousands of micro-decisions which need to be made while mountaineering. After putting on our crampons, Austin and I were faced with a fork in the road... Should we avoid the steeper, icier section of the Jampa Glacier by skirting around its flank? In the bottom of the photo, you can see a rope team who took this route. Austin and I elected for the more direct route up the heart of the Jampa Glacier. Turns out we made the right decision - the unseasonably warm conditions had opened up crevasses on the circuitous route. We later found out that our friends who took the alternative route had fallen into a crevasse that morning.
rgb.photo.v1.5d9603b8-65e5-4557-aa47-126eb1b1a781	rgb.user.v1.ffb2fde6-fba6-40aa-8bb6-55b23d5d3c1d	photo	2019-11-23 20:29:31+00	2020-03-09 20:53:42.937+00	3	5062	3375	#215a92	{"bucket": "charliemcgrady-media-prod", "suffixes": {"lg": {"jpg": "_lg.jpg", "webp": "_lg.webp"}, "md": {"jpg": "_md.jpg", "webp": "_md.webp"}, "sm": {"jpg": "_sm.jpg", "webp": "_sm.webp"}, "xl": {"jpg": "_xl.jpg", "webp": "_xl.webp"}, "xs": {"jpg": "_xs.jpg", "webp": "_xs.webp"}, "xxl": {"jpg": "_xxl.jpg", "webp": "_xxl.webp"}, "xxs": {"jpg": "_xxs.jpg", "webp": "_xxs.webp"}}, "keyPrefix": "rgb.user.v1.ffb2fde6-fba6-40aa-8bb6-55b23d5d3c1d/rgb.photo.v1.5d9603b8-65e5-4557-aa47-126eb1b1a781/rgb.photo.v1.5d9603b8-65e5-4557-aa47-126eb1b1a781"}	Austin taking a brief brake on the summit crater. When I reflect on the emotions invoked by this photo, "celebratory" and "accomplished" fall pretty far down the list. The reality is we were both pretty scared knowing we had to descend the mile of ice we just came up. That's the irony of mountaineering - you never really can fully celebrate a climb until it's over.
