rgb.story.v1.c59d7abb-0e18-4a56-9219-c703cc10f04f	rgb.user.v1.ffb2fde6-fba6-40aa-8bb6-55b23d5d3c1d	2020-02-24 23:37:16+00	2020-03-20 15:58:27+00	Traversing Timberline Country	{"{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.ccfefb68-de48-4e9d-86b8-97c6361b0be5\\", \\"type\\": \\"text\\", \\"metadata\\": {\\"html\\": \\"<p class=\\\\\\"ql-align-center\\\\\\"><span class=\\\\\\"ql-size-huge\\\\\\">Traversing Timberline Country</span></p><p class=\\\\\\"ql-align-center\\\\\\"><em class=\\\\\\"ql-size-large\\\\\\">Thirty Days Alone In The Sierra Wilderness</em></p>\\"}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.454ced44-d3bc-46ff-bc18-9aa975084b68\\", \\"type\\": \\"media-grid\\", \\"media\\": [{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.94c5bad3-402f-456e-91e6-cb1e9e6514e3\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}], \\"metadata\\": {\\"makePhotosFullHeight\\": true}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.c84e1bcd-cf7b-4644-9ffb-4df93d18bff3\\", \\"type\\": \\"text\\", \\"metadata\\": {\\"html\\": \\"<p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\" class=\\\\\\"ql-size-large\\\\\\">DAY 1</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\">Charlie and I both flew into Fresno at ~10:30 AM on Saturday, August 17th. I had arranged a ride through craigslist with a retiree named Curtis who promised he would loop around the Arrivals exit every 10 minutes as he did not have a cell phone to reach him on. Sure enough, he arrived in his beat-up white Siena van right after we went outside. He took us to a Big 5 where we grabbed fuel for our stoves and grabbed burritos at a Mexican joint while Curtis waited in his van. Curtis was SUPER friendly, to the extent that he would be telling us a story about bowling a 270 or ripping vinyl LPs to disc that he would and then realize he was driving the wrong direction! I had to wake up at 3 AM to catch my connecting flight to Dallas so I was quite tired, and, sitting in shotgun, I would occasionally manage something like “cool” in response to Curtis’ tales – which was enough to spur him into other recitals. There are probably a thousand more things to say about our friend Curtis, but I must leave those musings for another time.&nbsp;</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\">We arrived at Road’s End ~2:30 PM. In my head the plan was to start hiking on Sunday and spend the night somewhere near the trailhead. Copper Creek trail, the Sierra High Route’s southern terminus, is rather unpopular compared to the other trails starting at Road’s End so we were able to switch our permits to start that Saturday. After chatting with a cute hobbit-looking ranger and signing the requisite paperwork Charlie and I said our farewells to Curtis and were soon on our way up the dusty trail.</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\">We were both tired from traveling and completely unacclimated so by the time we got to upper tent meadow we were feeling the altitude. This led to some interesting proclamations such as Charlie’s – “wow I forgot how much I hate hiking”. We made it the saddle where the cross country section of the Sierra High Route begins, scarfed down our food, and passed out. The nearly full waning moon casted a strange light on the Western Sierra that night.</span></p><p><br></p><p><br></p>\\"}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.d7375d58-e25e-4a3c-9f8c-20b740457e74\\", \\"type\\": \\"text\\", \\"metadata\\": {\\"html\\": \\"<p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\" class=\\\\\\"ql-size-large\\\\\\">DAY 2</span></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\"><span class=\\\\\\"ql-cursor\\\\\\">﻿</span></span></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\">We were feeling somewhat better in the morning and made decent progress. We started ~8 AM, were over Grouse Pass by 10 AM, and were over Goat Crest saddle by 11:30 AM. When we were on the eastern side of Goat Crest saddle we had our first encounter with John, a SHR solo through hiker. He was already talking about bailing in Bishop which seemed a bit grim that it hadn’t been more than 24 hours since he started. We let him pass us as we both took a shit break somewhere above Glacier valley – his itinerary was a bit more ambitious than the 21 days we allotted ourselves. Glacier valley was a pleasant tromp although we were coming to realize that there were more than “pockets” of mosquitoes. They we ubiquitous, we had forgotten mosquito head nets, and had only a small tube of DEET.</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\">We followed “faint” use trails to State Lakes, and the horseshoe lakes were not hard to find from there. A minor disaster struck when I realized the cap on my mini dr. bronner’s soap broke, and the brain of my bag was consequently covered in soap. I tried to wipe the slimy residue from items like my headlamp and put them in the spare food drybag in my bag’s main compartment. When we were approaching Windy Ridge Charlie decided he wanted to camp at Windy Point, at the north western end of Windy Ridge for some good sunset photography. This required us to traverse around a few “subpeaks” en route to Windy Ridge which seems a bit ridiculous to complain about since the whole thing was just sandy class 1, but it was a little tough after a full day, not fully acclimated. Compared to my solo trip in July, I had been feeling more beat up about recent affairs in my romantic life on this trip. Can we snuff the heart’s flame by robbing our minds of oxygen?</span></p><p><br></p><p><br></p>\\"}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.59b4f8c1-e64a-4e9f-8d13-43140d093aea\\", \\"type\\": \\"virtual-reality\\", \\"media\\": [{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photosphere.v1.3ae46c3a-9a9e-4b5c-95d8-79d46e48db74\\", \\"type\\": \\"photosphere\\"}], \\"metadata\\": {\\"scenes\\": [{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photosphere.v1.3ae46c3a-9a9e-4b5c-95d8-79d46e48db74\\", \\"name\\": \\"rgb.photosphere.v1.3ae46c3a-9a9e-4b5c-95d8-79d46e48db74\\", \\"levels\\": [{\\"size\\": 256, \\"tileSize\\": 256, \\"fallbackOnly\\": true}, {\\"size\\": 512, \\"tileSize\\": 512}, {\\"size\\": 1024, \\"tileSize\\": 512}, {\\"size\\": 2048, \\"tileSize\\": 512}], \\"faceSize\\": 2048, \\"initialViewParameters\\": {\\"fov\\": 1.297699278661344, \\"yaw\\": -0.11967972013675165, \\"pitch\\": 0.18417591719990334}}]}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.baa6fe9d-b549-417b-9033-94fa0c8b74e9\\", \\"type\\": \\"media-grid\\", \\"media\\": [{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.e641d9d5-064d-44f2-91cb-7436a964149b\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.36978b1d-4f4b-422d-a230-69c9a91d685f\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.eb4cc2dc-b435-4244-a5b7-33bbe06a892a\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.0bb6e2b8-fac2-4ecb-a579-07c02038e4d3\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.62b00b5d-9dbf-46d3-a740-6f853a8bd192\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.fae6e50d-2b39-475c-b9ff-72f60259e62d\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.aace5a23-2925-40ab-834b-ebb458d3f8f8\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.9f688a2c-6dec-4f45-8096-5e5b651f63db\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.f217515a-5f21-4190-bb8d-55172c9899f5\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.0c30880a-54c0-491f-9d3f-2b77ffc27b43\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}], \\"metadata\\": {}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.8a8b8f2a-1db0-4b0e-8b47-a4ff64ccf317\\", \\"type\\": \\"text\\", \\"metadata\\": {\\"html\\": \\"<p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\" class=\\\\\\"ql-size-large\\\\\\">DAY 3</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\">We got off Windy Point ~7:45 AM and then descended to Grey Pass. The meadows below Grey Pass at the start of the climb to White Pass were stunning, and the surrounding granite ramparts were more “classic Sierra” than the western slope we were leaving behind. We did a good job navigating to White Pass, and Red Pass was easy to get to from there. Marion Lake was the most stunningly blue lake I had ever seen. Charlie remarked that it was reminiscent of PNW glacier fed lakes.&nbsp;</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\">Above Marion Lake we were a bit tired, but we made our way across much talus to a cirque that we thought was the base of Frozen Lakes Pass. It looked like a complete deathtrap, class 3 most of the way up. We contemplated it for 15 minutes, and I decided to check on my GPS (we had only relied on compass/maps thus far). Alas, we were one cirque too far north. It was already 5 PM, but we decided to move to the lake below Frozen Lakes Pass. The actual pass looked </span><em style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\">somewhat </em><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\">easier than what we were dreading only an hour prior but still sketchy. I don’t think Charlie, who is otherwise the more experienced outdoorsman, has had a ton of experience on talus/boulders slopes so I think his nerves got to me. There was a certain tension as we started making camp, but it dissipated as we hung out while the sun set.</span></p><p><br></p><p><br></p>\\"}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.5bbcc7e2-21d7-4fa2-851b-69d1f2b8d5b8\\", \\"type\\": \\"virtual-reality\\", \\"media\\": [{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photosphere.v1.46ea8900-b8f1-4218-8a8d-f8b864d34961\\", \\"type\\": \\"photosphere\\"}], \\"metadata\\": {\\"scenes\\": [{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photosphere.v1.46ea8900-b8f1-4218-8a8d-f8b864d34961\\", \\"name\\": \\"rgb.photosphere.v1.46ea8900-b8f1-4218-8a8d-f8b864d34961\\", \\"levels\\": [{\\"size\\": 256, \\"tileSize\\": 256, \\"fallbackOnly\\": true}, {\\"size\\": 512, \\"tileSize\\": 512}, {\\"size\\": 1024, \\"tileSize\\": 512}, {\\"size\\": 2048, \\"tileSize\\": 512}], \\"faceSize\\": 2048, \\"initialViewParameters\\": {\\"fov\\": 1.372453905107795, \\"yaw\\": -0.7061103488068543, \\"pitch\\": -0.004146386420263326}}]}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.fc1bed4e-e6e5-42b9-868c-73a9765f30cd\\", \\"type\\": \\"media-grid\\", \\"media\\": [{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.b8ee9c06-5371-449b-898b-e248be811f79\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.09e66f04-6c7e-4b38-b160-1126c29c006b\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.23eba763-f8ae-4988-ae5e-b75deb68fbf9\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.f87a54f1-a526-4ace-8c6d-80cf5dc6c843\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.f0ecfbd3-8f77-44bb-b851-04d0d09e5b84\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.49ab6b88-7527-4319-910d-b03e204dcad0\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}], \\"metadata\\": {}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.6bc95e8c-df75-424f-88d6-92d39e99fd53\\", \\"type\\": \\"text\\", \\"metadata\\": {\\"html\\": \\"<p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\" class=\\\\\\"ql-size-large\\\\\\">DAY 4</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\">A classic day in the Sierra, and our first day in what I would consider the “Eastern Sierra”. We got over the relatively stable boulders on the eastern side of Frozen Lakes Pass, and descending was not terribly sketchy. From Frozen Lakes Pass we looked across the Upper Basin to Split Mountain, the first 14’er I had ever ascended (only the summer prior). The rock on it was looked more “brown-reddish” than the grey granite we stood atop – it gave it a “badlands-y” look. We were in the Upper Basin at the intersection with the JMT by 11:17 AM where Charlie found a herd of deer to chase/photograph. We encountered maybe a dozen people on the 6 miles between Mather Pass and Palisades Lakes, including some completely unabashed skinny dippers. Leaving behind the JMT traffic, we unwittingly entered the “crux” of the day – ascending a slightly confusing and seemingly never-ending array of ledges and slabs to get to the top of Cirque Pass. I am learning to appreciate slabs, but a few small falls tested my patience. As Roper promises, Potluck pass looked quite insurmountable from atop Cirque Pass, and the typical route up the western scree part seemed to be covered in snow.&nbsp;</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\">Charlie’s mattress pad was leaking the previous night before Frozen Lakes Pass, and our trip was doomed if we couldn’t fix it. Hard to imagine sleeping on granite every night and awaking to 12+ miles of largely talus hopping. Thankfully I had 2 “glue dot” that came with my mattress pad. We were able to find the minute holes on Charlie’s pad with soapy water and hoped the applied patch would hold. We pitched my tent on a granite slab for the second night in a row, and snow melt water moved all around us.&nbsp;</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\">At this point Charlie and I were having a bit of trip anxiety. The mosquitos and, I suppose, our uneven nerves made it hard to relax in one spot for too long. Rather than taking the 7 days to resupply from North Lake TH, we were leaning towards finishing this leg in 6. As well, we wanted to get over the Glacier Divide with one of the SHR’s arguably hardest passes, Snow Tounge Pass, before resupplying. Going over Snow Tounge with 7 days of food seemed like an unnecessary burden. Thus, we scrapped our tentative plans of going over Lamarck Col in favor of going over Piute Pass.</span></p><p><br></p><p><br></p>\\"}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.25fbe258-d387-4434-8083-7ab26cdfaf11\\", \\"type\\": \\"virtual-reality\\", \\"media\\": [{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photosphere.v1.009d2118-83af-40e8-b4d1-66268d974c9e\\", \\"type\\": \\"photosphere\\"}], \\"metadata\\": {\\"scenes\\": [{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photosphere.v1.009d2118-83af-40e8-b4d1-66268d974c9e\\", \\"name\\": \\"rgb.photosphere.v1.009d2118-83af-40e8-b4d1-66268d974c9e\\", \\"levels\\": [{\\"size\\": 256, \\"tileSize\\": 256, \\"fallbackOnly\\": true}, {\\"size\\": 512, \\"tileSize\\": 512}, {\\"size\\": 1024, \\"tileSize\\": 512}, {\\"size\\": 2048, \\"tileSize\\": 512}], \\"faceSize\\": 2048, \\"initialViewParameters\\": {\\"fov\\": 1.5707963267948966, \\"yaw\\": 0, \\"pitch\\": 0}}]}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.53fdf1f8-c72e-46cf-b848-cec7923890d6\\", \\"type\\": \\"media-grid\\", \\"media\\": [{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.865a9184-758a-487b-925c-a9613c4ac08b\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.2db074c1-0c55-421f-80c2-83fa859c2eb1\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.6c3eb33f-7e46-4f94-b641-7d4390fa1bd3\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.08d0d5a9-0b14-47c5-ab71-a2e318f7012f\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.143e307f-fe23-4039-a10f-4ce1c3149c9d\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.6e525c2d-739b-4c09-935b-49f7d9c5efc9\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.3c6eabca-90b4-415f-b090-60e2914e18ce\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.19e10438-9fbb-4cd4-8526-5bbbbe4fcc5e\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}], \\"metadata\\": {}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.be5dd1a6-a426-4236-ba2d-d67117324490\\", \\"type\\": \\"text\\", \\"metadata\\": {\\"html\\": \\"<p><span class=\\\\\\"ql-size-large\\\\\\" style=\\\\\\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;\\\\\\">DAY 5</span></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;\\\\\\">﻿</span></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;\\\\\\">Today was a hard day for Ziv. We started hiking right after 8 AM and immediately had to surmount Potluck Pass. As we had seen from Cirque Pass, the “unpleasant scree” to the left of the pass had snow on it so we instead gained the pass via the middle. There were a few class 3 moved involved and Charlie scouted ahead to see if the ledges “went”. After that we both shit somewhere in the not-too-hairy country below North Palisade, and then worked our way to Knapsack Pass which I think we made a bit harder than it had to be. We descended into Dusy Basin and skinny dipped in a slightly slime-y lake – West Coast slime, albeit. We found Bishop Pass Trail and had a casual descent to where it intersects with the JMT. We lounged in a large “camp” area right on the JMT. I was reading the “Shakespeare: An Overview” introductory section of my Penguin Classic copy of King Lear. That and the Introduction proper would take me most of the trip to finish. We hiked for maybe an hour or two more that day and camped slightly above Big Pete Meadow.&nbsp;</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;\\\\\\">I had been having remarkably vivid dreams. I had a dream that it was my birthday and there was some sort of cool dance at a fast-food place. Paul, my roommate, gave me a cool collage featuring the band Failure. My uncle and aunt (Margie &amp; John) took me to get smoothies and onion rings. I don’t think any birthday I’ve ever had can hold a candle to that.</span></p>\\"}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.9394968a-0766-473b-8928-cc3e58c6d202\\", \\"type\\": \\"media-grid\\", \\"media\\": [{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.3a00ec75-915e-4b6d-b4c5-561fa5332ab0\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.73e15908-595f-4455-a8fb-61a2fffbeef5\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.9e1d2f02-777b-4152-8643-919706906cdc\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}], \\"metadata\\": {}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.579e6484-a62d-4af8-86e3-b5c1679516bd\\", \\"type\\": \\"text\\", \\"metadata\\": {\\"html\\": \\"<p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\" class=\\\\\\"ql-size-large\\\\\\">DAY 6</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\">Sometimes the biggest events of the day happen before you even get out of the tent. I awoke to some scratching noise near our tent and opened my eyes to a large black bear staring at me through the mesh of the tent. I started hollering which awoke Charlie. We both jumped out of the tent and chased the bear further away. My cooking pot was dented and my bear canister covered in bear slime, but the larger casualty was Charlie’s platypus which meant we were out 2.5 liters of water storage capacity. This was my first bear encounter in the Sierra.</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\">As we were walking further north in Kings Canyon on the JMT we met our friend Jon who we had first encountered several days prior below Goat Crest Saddle. Turns out he had re-supplied over Bishop Pass. Jon was my personal savior as he found my hiking pole that I left somewhere on the grass slope above Evolution Lake while taking a shit. Charlie seemed to have a good time discussing his post-Amazon life. Together, Charlie, Jon, and I began the next off-trail section of the SHR which began with a largely wooded and sometimes bush-whacky ascent to a beautiful grassy valley before Snow Tongue Pass.&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\"><span class=\\\\\\"ql-cursor\\\\\\">﻿</span></span></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\">Jon seemed a bit defeated by end of the day, but I was feeling stronger which was enough to put me in a good mood. Jon seemed dialed in with his gear and had some good tips like “dehydrated towels”. </span></p><p><br></p><p><br></p>\\"}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.bacae7b9-eb7d-4d31-a96e-774a3321d8cb\\", \\"type\\": \\"media-grid\\", \\"media\\": [{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.94c5bad3-402f-456e-91e6-cb1e9e6514e3\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.b035fbcd-4bdc-41cf-bcb6-9817a9ff1993\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.1186976b-c850-46df-b1ed-c68ca30d75cd\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}], \\"metadata\\": {}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.cc93cd3f-015f-4979-9a25-3ce52b8a2c72\\", \\"type\\": \\"text\\", \\"metadata\\": {\\"html\\": \\"<p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\" class=\\\\\\"ql-size-large\\\\\\">DAY 7</span></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\"><span class=\\\\\\"ql-cursor\\\\\\">﻿</span></span></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\">A big morning going over Snow Tongue Pass. Despite its infamy, the descent was fun, and the \\\\\\"endless talus\\\\\\" thereafter still enjoyable. We said our goodbyes to Jon in Humphrey's Basin - he had to make it to Red's Meadow in a few days. We veered right to what I think was the right choice for a first re-supply point, Piute Pass TH. Charlie and I made it to Piute Pass TH by 2:45 PM, and then walked a bit further down the road to Bishop Pack Outfitters. Meeting the owner, Mike, was certainly the highlight of the day. He was friendly, offering to let us stay at a small campsite on his property, and overall seemed like a real son-of-a-bitch. He poked Charlie’s stomach and asked why he didn’t lose his gut from hiking so much. Mike and at least two of his ranch hands offered us beer, which we declined as Charlie was in the midst of his sober year and I was anxious to return to the backcountry. We camped that night just a bit up the trail we came down, and enjoyed the atavistic charms of a campfire.</span></p><p><br></p><p><br></p>\\"}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.8cf0d595-e4be-4848-b901-0457a1d2f98e\\", \\"type\\": \\"media-grid\\", \\"media\\": [{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.bb3b06cb-c336-4689-a6a8-cd94b77f5443\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}], \\"metadata\\": {\\"makePhotosFullHeight\\": true}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.3640a031-8066-4c75-909f-77584d9fe888\\", \\"type\\": \\"media-grid\\", \\"media\\": [{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.3e36bc7b-d1a0-4837-b18a-bc195e9cfd8a\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.bad35444-44a9-41f4-9515-5563178d1095\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}], \\"metadata\\": {}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.d7bb5e93-2918-4a45-938b-e1cf8fafe40b\\", \\"type\\": \\"text\\", \\"metadata\\": {\\"html\\": \\"<p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\" class=\\\\\\"ql-size-large\\\\\\">DAY 8</span></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\"><span class=\\\\\\"ql-cursor\\\\\\">﻿</span></span></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\">Today followed pretty much the same day 1 route of my first solo backpacking trip in 2016, but I was not crying from exhaustion by the end. Rather, Charlie and rolled up to Merriam Lake at ~3:15 PM and had a nice skinny dip. That morning we crushed it over Piute Pass, but due to some navigational issues in Humphrey’s Basin weren’t at Puppet Pass until 11:50 AM. The French Canyon was disgustingly mosquito-infested. At this point in the trip my shoulders were covered in overlapping mosquito bites – it looked like a rash. A thunderhead was forming over Mount Humphrey’s late that afternoon, but it dissolved “impotently” per Roper’s apt description of the summertime meteorological phenomena.</span></p><p><br></p><p><br></p>\\"}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.42cad4f3-24cf-43e7-b479-270f34df645d\\", \\"type\\": \\"media-grid\\", \\"media\\": [{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.8b1f93b1-6a27-4213-8836-a852b2a0160d\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}], \\"metadata\\": {\\"makePhotosFullHeight\\": true}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.3f326f32-4a23-4879-9c09-d08326cca99a\\", \\"type\\": \\"media-grid\\", \\"media\\": [{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.aa47bbfe-f874-44cd-9174-24664dc1d527\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.e4f4e5f4-9ed7-4c83-b90d-7432048cd753\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.ee68a01c-1815-4d2e-b738-3393df74cf11\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.83519cfe-68f8-46c7-b229-18355a6ac2c0\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.b86a90f6-a33d-4ba9-bbce-0a79917d7da7\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}], \\"metadata\\": {}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.2f3ddf56-1cdf-4cb0-bf14-ab0855b79830\\", \\"type\\": \\"text\\", \\"metadata\\": {\\"html\\": \\"<p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\" class=\\\\\\"ql-size-large\\\\\\">DAY 9</span></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\"><span class=\\\\\\"ql-cursor\\\\\\">﻿</span></span></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\">This was a bit of a strange day. We didn’t have many miles planned and were a bit restless slowly making our way over Feather Pass and through the Bear Lakes region. Seven Gables looked indomitable but is apparently a mostly class 2 ascent. We hung out on the cliffs overlooking White Bear Lake until late in the afternoon and then decided to descend White Bear Pass. We had a great campsite below Teddy Bear Lake. As the sun set, it almost seemed that we could peer into the Central Valley of California with the Hilgard Branch of Bear Creek sparkling in the foreground. A steady breeze blew into my face as I stood transfixed by this illusion.</span></p><p><br></p><p><br></p>\\"}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.0dcc5719-0872-481a-9ef8-111052570f14\\", \\"type\\": \\"text\\", \\"metadata\\": {\\"html\\": \\"<p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\" class=\\\\\\"ql-size-large\\\\\\">DAY 10</span></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\"><span class=\\\\\\"ql-cursor\\\\\\">﻿</span></span></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\\\\\\">Definitely a “business” day. We surmounted both the Mono divide (via Gabbot Pass) and the Silver divide (via Bighorn and Shout-of-Relief passes). Charlie wanted some nice sunset pictures, so we ended up camping on top of Shout-of-Relief pass. As we made our way toward Bighorn Pass through the valley below Laurel Lake, Red &amp; White mountain appeared impossibly huge before us, and the view of Recess Peak in the opposite direction was awesome as well. Charlie and I were both pretty beat at Laurel Lake, but made good time over Bighorn Pass and Shout-of-relief pass. This was the last section of the Sierra High Route I had done </span></p><p><br></p><p><br></p>\\"}}"}
rgb.story.v1.7a9c611c-2aaf-4360-8c73-3a0d07ac0310	rgb.user.v1.ffb2fde6-fba6-40aa-8bb6-55b23d5d3c1d	2020-02-18 04:24:10+00	2020-03-26 15:09:54+00	The Highline	{"{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.eb1a4e17-0af5-46e9-8188-1aecd05134ab\\", \\"type\\": \\"text\\", \\"metadata\\": {\\"html\\": \\"<h1 class=\\\\\\"ql-align-center\\\\\\">Book Manuscript</h1>\\"}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.8177d4d8-4221-4b21-8ba8-f4ce25922598\\", \\"type\\": \\"text\\", \\"metadata\\": {\\"html\\": \\"<h1 class=\\\\\\"ql-align-center\\\\\\">Mountaineering</h1><p class=\\\\\\"ql-align-center\\\\\\"><br></p><p>The draw of the mountains. Where you find glory, return home a warrior.</p><ul><li>Grandfather.</li><li>Exploration of risk.</li><li>Tell the story of the flake breaking off and flying right by you.</li></ul>\\"}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.cd80d2f6-1058-43cf-a063-6f30c8b13a35\\", \\"type\\": \\"text\\", \\"metadata\\": {\\"html\\": \\"<h1 class=\\\\\\"ql-align-center\\\\\\">Tele-Landscape</h1><p class=\\\\\\"ql-align-center\\\\\\"><br></p><p>My favorite lenses are telephotos. You can zoom in and compress miles of terrain in a single frame.</p>\\"}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.aea970cc-7282-412d-ac1f-c9e12eca29a0\\", \\"type\\": \\"text\\", \\"metadata\\": {\\"html\\": \\"<h1 class=\\\\\\"ql-align-center\\\\\\">The Cosmos</h1>\\"}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.ab657e7d-0689-4360-8581-6d0006dc883a\\", \\"type\\": \\"text\\", \\"metadata\\": {\\"html\\": \\"<h1 class=\\\\\\"ql-align-center\\\\\\">Satellite Imagery</h1>\\"}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.e6a0bd7e-e812-4d6f-97ea-8168aa1f589d\\", \\"type\\": \\"text\\", \\"metadata\\": {\\"html\\": \\"<h1 class=\\\\\\"ql-align-center\\\\\\">Macro / Abstract Close Images</h1>\\"}}"}
rgb.story.v1.cfbeb188-05f1-4061-a6df-6ab3a1bef423	rgb.user.v1.ffb2fde6-fba6-40aa-8bb6-55b23d5d3c1d	2020-02-24 23:37:44+00	2020-03-05 19:24:21+00	The Enchanted Valley	{"{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.3b7484d9-f93a-4e97-be6e-05854e3ec798\\", \\"type\\": \\"text\\", \\"metadata\\": {\\"html\\": \\"<p class=\\\\\\"ql-align-center\\\\\\"><span class=\\\\\\"ql-size-huge\\\\\\">The Enchanted Valley</span></p><p class=\\\\\\"ql-align-center\\\\\\"><em class=\\\\\\"ql-size-large\\\\\\">Standing on the shoulders of giants</em></p>\\"}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.5cf73bab-f52c-4e40-ae44-e37cc9ccd185\\", \\"type\\": \\"media-grid\\", \\"media\\": [{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.442cf8a9-e37b-44a0-aecd-8c87cb69b313\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}], \\"metadata\\": {\\"targetRowHeight\\": 555}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.df9a9811-3aee-4372-a729-30f0cbd8622e\\", \\"type\\": \\"text\\", \\"metadata\\": {\\"html\\": \\"<p>The Enchanted Valley is tucked away in a remote corner of the Olympic Peninsula. It is a magic place - unfathomably large trees (including the worlds largest Western Hemlock) cascade from steep cliffs reaching into the clouds. Black bears roam the landscape foraging for berries and elk lazily graze below cliffs of granite. Continuing up the valley, you arrive at the base of Mount Anderson, a mighty granite peak in stark contrast to the lush rainforest below.</p>\\"}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.3d6b9142-7293-4a60-bf03-5f483a6944a9\\", \\"type\\": \\"media-grid\\", \\"media\\": [{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.2eef2cab-999d-4d6b-a21e-ea16f948a471\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.9138ff4e-a367-4eb9-9512-7283313935b6\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.420f70e8-f918-4ed2-ab0f-b626cd6afda4\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.ea497a5c-b07c-4add-ad61-e245c0ec2572\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.0baaf6e1-1d85-4ea9-aebb-087257ca5c41\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.b6e76ba5-0038-4e64-848b-4c0168d2d558\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.960a57c7-b217-4d07-add4-7b21cef4f043\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.afdf6ab1-e27e-4a3b-bde6-734f2a959887\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.4da6a593-c7ee-4cc5-802b-83badfb727d0\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}], \\"metadata\\": {\\"makePhotosFullHeight\\": true}}","{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.widget.v1.0a75b510-a382-4f01-b380-e99c8912d9e4\\", \\"type\\": \\"media-grid\\", \\"media\\": [{\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.22b89436-e725-4f56-8a84-ee8fdb161fba\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.ff17985a-93dc-4646-b6ae-8442238df9e2\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.442cf8a9-e37b-44a0-aecd-8c87cb69b313\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.21792729-acf2-4229-8739-8d397b2e3c36\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.70928c5b-ed97-411b-96c5-bb9b2b0eb3c1\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.2eef2cab-999d-4d6b-a21e-ea16f948a471\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.9138ff4e-a367-4eb9-9512-7283313935b6\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.420f70e8-f918-4ed2-ab0f-b626cd6afda4\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.eb79d1c7-3f44-455d-8dcb-713cf7237db0\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.b6e76ba5-0038-4e64-848b-4c0168d2d558\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.0baaf6e1-1d85-4ea9-aebb-087257ca5c41\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.d18b4cf0-93cf-4fc3-bdf3-e3301388c9cb\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.ea497a5c-b07c-4add-ad61-e245c0ec2572\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.2cc526f5-bf85-4b41-9b1c-c65c4e665ec2\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.ad97fa83-94e8-4fe5-84ca-be36d0372f3c\\", \\"type\\": \\"photo\\"}, {\\"id\\": \\"rgb.photo.v1.afdf6ab1-e27e-4a3b-bde6-734f2a959887\\", \\"typ